Nextvi 7.4                      August 29, 2026                     Nextvi 7.4

NEXTVI PATCHES
==============
Nextvi features a robust, context aware and customizable patching system.
patch2vi support program facilitates script transformations.

Apply one: ./name.sh
Apply a stack: ./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh
Each script applies itself, adds its name to $P2VI_PATCH and hands the
rest on, so a patch carrying a compat block for another sees it.
Assert what the chain did not run itself:
P2VI_PATCH="lsp.sh visual.sh" ./splits.sh
Every line below is one full chain, in an order that applies.
Patches not listed are unconstrained. Combinations are generated
by compat_order in patch2vi.sh.

./lsp.sh visual.sh arrowkeys_normal.sh
./grep.sh lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh cxref.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh incsearch.sh detect_indent.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh incsearch.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh linewrap_v2.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh linewrap_v2.sh mouse_normal.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh arrowkeys_insert.sh incsearch.sh detect_indent.sh tab_complete.sh
./rstr.sh threaded_search.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh

patch2vi.sh
--------------
Maintainer utils.

64bit.sh
--------------
Convert entire codebase to 64 bit integers instead of 32 bit.
This will enable Nextvi to read files & buffers up to 9,223,372 Terabytes.
In addition, this may improve performance on 64 bit CPUs due to
matching pointer <---> integer alignment and possibly more compiler
optimizations.
Run this after you've applied the patches.

gen-widechars.sh
--------------
A script I use to update zwchars and dwchars in uc.c

arrowkeys_normal.sh
--------------
Adds arrows keys support in normal mode.

arrowkeys_insert.sh
--------------
Adds arrows keys support in insert and command modes. In command
modes, Up/Down arrow selects lines from history buffer.

threaded_search.sh
--------------
Multithreaded version of lbuf_search(). Adjust the number of threads in use
and buffer stepping in lbuf.c Ensure that your system supports posix threads,
aka libpthread.

async.sh
--------------
Adds 2 new ex commands "as" and "as!". Ex command executed in command chain after
"as" is run on a separate thread. "as!" is a join command that waits for all threads
to finish. Posix thread support is required. Patch is intended for expert users because
races are possible depending on commands used.
Example: fun :fd command without blocking the editor
:as:fd

hl_part.sh
--------------
Allow syntax highlight to continue past the terminal dimensions up to a
certain limit. To customize the depth, adjust LEDFORW and LEDBACK definitions
in led.c
Patch will alter the meaning of <^> <$> assertions in syntax highlight
regex, as the line would be considered to start/end beyond the visible columns.
Patch works in reverse direction and with reordered text.

hl_naive.sh
--------------
Allow syntax highlight to continue past the terminal dimensions unlimited.
Performance will degrade on very long lines.

blockhl_cache.sh
--------------
Cache multiline syntax highlighting blocks. Falls outside of the scope
of core Nextvi as it violates the immediate mode rendering principle and
is more complex.

debug.sh
--------------
Cleanup reachable memory upon termination.
Use for debugging.
Note: in some features leaks won't be freed - for example hlw (which is not
enabled by default) Also initializes sdense in regex.c so that valgrind stops
freaking out.

grep.sh
--------------
Adds a special temporary buffer /grep/ much like the default /hist/ or /fm/.
When new keybind ^x is executed vi performs fs_search on every file and
stores the results in /grep/ buffer. At last, user will be switched to /grep/.
Line information is stored and displayed with special new syntax highlight
rules. Searched keyword is also highlighted.  Use the familiar keybind ^i or
TAB on the wanted line to immediately open the file and goto the line
specified. To quickly open /grep/ use new keybind: vx
The format of the lines stored is specifically designed to mirror the output of
grep -r -n "str" .
The feature can be substituted with a pipe command in case of a higher performing
grep implementation or loading of cached results from script.

cxref.sh
--------------
Adds a special temporary buffer /xref/ and the keybind gx, for C only.
gx collects the definition of the symbol under the cursor and, recursively,
the definitions of everything it calls, then switches to /xref/. A count
limits how deep it goes: 1gx is the definition alone, 2gx adds what it
calls, no count means unlimited. To quickly open /xref/ use new keybind:
vf Every definition gets a header line in grep -n style, followed by its
code verbatim, highlighted as C. TAB on a header opens that file at that
line, which is where you edit: /xref/ itself is only for reading. Definitions
are looked up in the C files listed in /fm/, so run :fd first, and the
file the call was read in is searched first, so a static definition beats
a global one elsewhere.

rstr.sh
--------------
Adaptation from upstream Neatvi, where simple operations are optimized by
running a specialized search code instead of full regex engine. rstr can
yeild up to 30% faster search on non regex patterns. This is not included in
base nextvi due to underlying complexity.

incsearch.sh
--------------
Search as you go in the / and ? prompts. Every typed key redraws the screen
with the matches of the keyword highlighted and the cursor moved to the first
match, scrolling the buffer when the match is off screen. Enter keeps that
position and searches normally, so counts and messages are unchanged. An
interrupt, or an erase key on an already empty keyword, restores the view,
the previous keyword and the search direction. While in the prompt TAB moves
to the next match and ^_ moves in reverse.

stdin_pipe.sh
--------------
Allows the user to use vi like a pager (less) except you need to specify
- as command line arg (just like in vim).
Example:
$ dmesg | vi -

ec_closebuf.sh
--------------
Allows the user to close the buffer using new ex command "cx"
Specify a number as an arg for what buffer to close.
Specifying no argument(s) will close the current buffer.
When the buffer is closed all buffers above will be shifted
and renumbered.
Range can be supplied like so :cx0-5
This will close buffers 0 to 5 inclusive
Omiting the parameter of the range assumes current buffer
index for that parameter (not inclusive and either begin or end
of the range). Example :cx-5 or :cx5-

filetype_shebang.sh
--------------
Detect and set the corresponding filetype based on the shebang
or specific data in the file. Currently supports only shell
and python.

kbd_layout.sh
--------------
Changes hjkl to work well with other keyboard layouts.
In this example hjkl is changed to yneo (workman keyboard layout)
Note: this patch can be used as a base for remapping any base keybinds.

quickexit.sh
--------------
Added kj sequence to quickly exit insert mode.
It's important to understand that this has a caveat, if input comes too fast,
for example a paste from terminal and the sequence has "kj" in it, insert
mode will be cancelled mid way and paste will be wrong. Luckily this almost
never happens because such letter combination is extremely rare.
Delay time (in ms) can be set via :qe N or disabled by setting N to 0.

hl_mark.sh
-------------
Adds new ex option "hlm" which will highlight lbuf marks. Each mark has
different color up to 16. Higher marks may duplicate the colors.
Lazy redraw, use <^L> if needed.

c_option.sh
-------------
Adds -c commandline option. This can be used to pass in ex commands instead
of EXINIT.

exrc.sh
-------------
Adds support for reading .exrc file from XDG, .config or home directories.
This can be used to pass in ex commands instead of EXINIT.

readonly.sh
-------------
Adds new ex option "ro" and commandline readonly option -R

remove_vla_cliterals.sh
--------------
Removes variable length arrays and compound literals for portability reasons.

undojump.sh
--------------
Adds a new keybind ^o which allows to jump through the undo marks
without actually doing undo.

undo_branches.sh
--------------
Adds a basic multi-level undo implementation. New ex command "up"
prints divergent changes. Each path has an index, switching to it
with "up" and a number as an argument. Example: :up1
The current undo branch is stored as the last index before swapping.

alternate-w-behaviour.sh
--------------
Implements more vim/like d/c/yw

ac_context.sh
--------------
Improvements to autocomplete, now the word completion will also use
text that prefixes the insert when it makes sense. Makes it possible
to autocomplete in more desired situations.

tab_complete.sh
--------------
Adds path completion to the ex prompt, bound to TAB. The word before
the cursor is completed as a path, relative to the working directory
or to the directory that the word itself names. A single match is
filled in, with a trailing </> when it is a directory. Several matches
fill in their common prefix, and once nothing is left to fill in, they
are listed. With "tc" set to 1 the list is drawn next to the prompt,
the cursor left where it was, so the prompt never scrolls. With "tc"
set to 2 the screen is cleared and the list is drawn below the prompt
instead, wrapped at word boundaries; TAB then pages it and any other
key leaves it. New keybind ^_ completes like "tc" set to 2 whatever
"tc" is, so the full listing is always one key away.
When nothing matches, TAB inserts a literal tab.
Adds a new ex option "tc" to toggle the completion, on by default.
Example: turn it off, TAB is then always a literal tab
:tc 0

linewrap_v1.sh
--------------
Soft line wrap implementation using buffer only approach.
Adds new :lw command which can be used to specify the max column
to which soft linewrap lines. Adds a new ex option "hllw" to
highlight linewrapped boundaries.
Trades off flexibility for simplicity. Cannot change linewrapping
when buffer is modified. Different edit semantics. Visual edits
are line based.

linewrap_v2.sh
--------------
Soft line wrap implementation using render only approach.
Adds new :lw command which can be used to specify the max column
to which soft linewrap lines. Adds a new ex option "hllw" to
highlight linewrapped boundaries.
No limitation from v1. Default edit semantics. Visual edits are
geometry based.

ex-scripting.sh
--------------
Adds new ex commands "sr" and "sx"
NEXTVI_FT - current buffer filetype
NEXTVI_ROW - current line number
NEXTVI_OFF - current byte offset
NEXTVI_COFF - current character offset
NEXTVI_LINE - current line
NEXTVI_WORD - current word
"sr" runs a command with the environment variables above
"sx" runs a command with the environment variables above and
executes its stdout as ex commands

chunked_read_write.sh
--------------
Allows nextvi to read files in chunks instead of reading the whole
file into the memory right away. This patch optimizes the reading of
binary files, since it would be truncated earlier before loading
the whole file into memory.
In addition, optimizes the number of write syscalls using chunks
when writing a file.

visual.sh
--------------
Adds character-wise (gv), line-wise (gV), and block (gb) visual selection
modes. Move the cursor to extend the selection; the selected region is
highlighted with inverted colors. Press the same key again to exit
that mode; ESC also cancels the selection.
The current mode (-- VISUAL --, -- VISUAL LINE --, -- VISUAL BLOCK --)
appears in the ^g status bar; when the permanent status bar is enabled
it shows continuously alongside the file information.
Supported operators applied to the selection:
  y  yank          d  delete         c/C/S  change
  >  indent        <  dedent         !  pipe through shell command
  ~  toggle case   u  lowercase      U  uppercase
  :  enter ex prompt with a scoped range
Block mode (gb) also supports:
  I  insert at the left edge of the block on every selected line;
     lines shorter than the left edge are skipped
  A  append after the right edge of the block on every selected line;
     for lines shorter than the right edge, text is appended at EOL

splits.sh
--------------
Implements "sp" and "vs" ex commands for vertical and horizontal splits.
Adds vc and vd normal commands to switch and close views. View can also be closed
with "q".
New ex command "eq" can be used to balance the vertical and horizontal
split space in the nearest dimension.

alt_sections.sh
--------------
Implements closer to standard vi(1) sections keybinding:
[#])          Move to next sentence boundary down # times
[#](          Move to next sentence boundary up # times
[#]]          Move to next <{> section down # times
[#][          Move to next <{> section up # times
[#]}          Move to next <Newline> section down # times
[#]{          Move to next <Newline> section up # times

key_remap_cmds.sh
--------------
Adds "im" "im!" and "nm "nm!" ex commands to dynamically remap keybinds
in normal and insert modes.
Remapping a to hello in insert mode
:im ahello
Remapping a to i in normal mode
:nm ai

modal.sh
--------------
Integrates modal programming language interpreter.
https://wiki.xxiivv.com/site/modal.html
New ex command [vrange]md[script] evaluates the contents of the
current buffer and argument as a modal script.
Implements a new phase - ($ ...) to execute ex command
chains from within the modal script.

lsp.sh
--------------
LSP support for Nextvi
Adds new ex command "lsp" to start the LSP server, for example:
:lsp c clangd
Check that it is running:
:lsp
Once the server is up, new normal keybinds can be used:
gK - show hover documentation for the symbol at cursor
gd - goto definition of symbol at cursor
Also supports on the fly hinting.

sse.sh
--------------
SIMD optimizations for Nextvi.

mouse_normal.sh
--------------
Adds mouse support in normal mode. Adds a new ex option "ms"
to toggle the mouse support. Clicking positions the cursor
and mouse wheel scrolls using ^Y or ^E. Terminal mouse selections
can still be made while holding Shift on most terminal emulators.

detect_indent.sh
--------------
Scans lines to guess the indentation format.
Adds 3 new ex options:
"et" - expandtab. Tabs are replaced with spaces
"sw" - space width. How many literal spaces are injected for tab
"idt" - indent detect. Enables indent guessing and sets the line
limit (default 500).

mhash.sh
--------------
A hash map implementation for marks which aims to take O(n) mark
search down to ~O(1). An exercise. Unjustified complexity for
regular use.

writeall.sh
--------------
Adds "wa" "wa!" "wqa" "wqa!" "xa" "xa!" ex commands to write all buffers
to the filesystem. q commands always write while x commands only
write modified buffers.

ins_cursor.sh
--------------
Changes cursor to a thin vertical bar when in insert mode.
This is a good indicator for insert mode.

cbas.sh
--------------
Adds syntax highlighing for Coders Basilisk
https://codeberg.org/gek/coders_basilisk/src/branch/main

highlight_cmd.sh
--------------
Adds a new ex command "hi". This command can be used to create
additional syntax highlighting rules on demand. It inherits
full power of attributes so any mask will work. Figuring out
the right value requires some effort and reading vi.h helps.
Example: highlight int as keyword (RE color)
:1hi int
Example: highlight multiple different keywords
:0,1,5,3hi \<(int)|(void)|(if)\>
Example: disable rules
:hi
Example: highlight text inside parens
:5hi \(.*?\)

My current EXINIT setup:
--------------
How it works:
|sc!||1sc | - disable special characters
97reg - put macro into a register
ish - enable interactive shell
inc (^(?:(?!^\.git).)+[^/]+$) always exclude .git folder from :fd
pac - print autocomplete options
|sc|? - default special characters; ? is a nop cycle required for
separator reset. Scripts can append more commands to EXINIT.

Macros:
@q = creates structure like this:
{

}
@w = creates structure like this:
whatever {
	whatever
	a
}
@e = brings :e !p $c into ex prompt
Note: p is alias to printf. $c[0-9] is env var containing some path
@a = creates a comment like this:
/* whatever */
@s = from the cursor creates a comment like this:
/* whatever */
@d = uncomment /* */ comment
@x = indent % region left
@f = brings :cd !p $c into ex prompt
Note: p is alias to printf. $c[0-9] is env var containing some path
@l = bring !cp % %_|w|!git diff --no-index %_ % macro into ex prompt.
Use to create patch and diff files fast. It relies on
you not saving the file prior to running the macro.
@z = control character mapping placeholders
@t = working with ./tags generated by ctags
and goto definition based on the current regex keyword
@r = goto definition in while being in ./tags file

export EXINIT="$(printf '%b' '|sc!||1sc |:108reg :!cp % %_:w:!git diff --no-index %_ %113reg o{\n}kA\n	115reg i/*A*/119reg A {jo}koa120reg >%<<$%<<122reg :ph1 1 2 1^A:ph2 2 2 1^B:ph3 3 2 1^C:ph4 4 2 1^D:ph5 5 2 1^E:ph6 6 2 1^F:ph7 7 2 1^G:ph8 8 2 1^H:ph9 9 2 1^I:ph10 10 2 1^J:ph11 11 2 1^K:ph12 12 2 1^L:ph13 13 2 1^M:ph14 14 2 1^N:ph15 15 2 1^O:ph16 16 2 1^P:ph17 17 2 1^Q:ph18 18 2 1^R:ph19 19 2 1^S:ph20 20 2 1^T:ph21 21 2 1^U:ph22 22 2 1^V:ph23 23 2 1^W:ph24 24 2 1^X:ph25 25 2 1^Y:ph26 26 2 1^Z:ph27 27 2 1^[:ph28 28 2 1^\\\\:ph29 29 2 1^]:ph30 30 2 1^^:ph31 31 2 1^_102reg :cd !p $c101reg :e !p $c97reg I/*A*/100reg /\/\*\n2x/\*\/\n2x116reg :51reg %@47:e ./tags:1;0:;>unescaped>:??!\%s/[\\\\](.)/\\1/g\:1i unescaped:ic:%f>^%@51	:??!p "%@51" not found in tags\:ic\:e %#\:re %@51\:1q:;>	>+1;#>	>ya49:;>	/\^>+3;#>\$/;">re:??!;>	[0-9]+>+1;#>;>ya50\:??\!p bad format\\\\\:ic\\\\\:e %#\\\\\:re %@51\\\\\:2q\:e %@49\:?\%@50\:re %@51\:ic\:1q:e %@49:1:%f>:re %@51:ic114reg :51reg %@47:;>	>+1;#>	>ya49:;>	/\^>+3;#>\$/;">re:??!;>	[0-9]+>+1;#>;>ya50\:??\!p bad format\\\\\:re %@51\\\\\:2q\:e %@49\:?\%@50\:re %@51\:1q:e %@49:1:ic:%f>:re %@51:icinc (^(?:(?!^\.git).)+[^/]+$)pacish|sc|?')"
